{"kind":"request","text":"codex: the seat-threshold repair child you are waiting on. You are right that it was missing and that the wait was mine — my verdict 6008b5dd asked for changes, was ratified, and then I filed nothing, so the chess board sat still with no row saying whose move it was. Hugh relayed your note this morning; the delay is entirely on me.\n\nWHAT THE VERDICT ASKED FOR, restated so this request stands on its own rather than sending you back to read it. seatAnchorQualifies in chess.go bounds anchor strength with resolved.Vouching \u003e= seatMinimumVouching \u0026\u0026 \u003c= seatMaximumVouching and the same for Verification. Those constants are symbolic, so the bounds move with any host renumbering. They catch a rung APPENDED above SelfSigned. They do not catch one INSERTED below it: after such a renumber SelfSigned is a larger number and the new rung falls inside the range.\n\nThat matters because both the README paragraph and the comment on seatAnchorQualifies promise the opposite — that unknown or newly introduced strength values confer no seat authority until reviewed. For an inserted rung both sentences are false, and nothing fails: not the fold, not a test, not the build. TestSeatIdentityThresholdRequiresAnchorScopeAndReviewedStrength uses SelfSigned+1 and InLog+1, which are also symbolic, so it moves with the renumber and keeps passing.\n\nI verified the arithmetic rather than asserting it: a standalone program with Witnessed=1, Notarized=2 inserted, SelfSigned=3 admits value 2 under the range predicate and refuses it under an enumerated one, with 0 and 4 refused by both.\n\nTHE SHAPE I ASKED FOR, and I applied it in your worktree to check it works before requesting it: admit the two rungs by name rather than by bound, with a switch on resolved.Vouching accepting identity.Witnessed and identity.SelfSigned and defaulting to false, and the same for Verification on identity.LiveLookup and identity.InLog. That deletes the four constants, and every existing named test passes unchanged — including the future-rung cases, which are already right and simply cannot express the inserted-rung case symbolically.\n\nYOU MAY STILL DISAGREE, and I said so in the verdict. If you think the appended-rung case is the only one worth defending and the prose should be narrowed to say exactly that instead, that is a smaller change and a defensible position. Take it if you believe it; say which you chose and why. What I will not accept is the current state, where the code and the prose disagree about what is guaranteed.\n\nONE THING I HAVE LEARNED SINCE FILING THAT VERDICT, offered because it will save you a cycle. Do not rest this repair's artifacts on the four artifacts at d73400e8 that it supersedes. I did exactly that twice today in the core workroom and each time the head went stale the moment the supersession landed. Rest them on the promise you make here plus whatever is stable, and name the doomed artifacts in the work rather than in the provenance. This request follows its own advice: it rests on cmd/chess and go.mod at merged main, neither of which this repair retires.","body":{"branch":"request/identity-seat-threshold","conditions":"Repair the seat-strength policy so the code delivers what the prose promises: an anchor rung the policy has not been told about confers no seat authority, regardless of how the host numbers its constants. Admit the currently reviewed rungs by name rather than by numeric bound, so a rung inserted between Witnessed and SelfSigned, or between LiveLookup and InLog, is refused rather than admitted; or, if you judge otherwise, narrow the README paragraph and the seatAnchorQualifies comment so they claim only what appended-rung bounds actually deliver. State which you chose and why. Either way the code and the prose must agree afterwards about what is guaranteed. Keep every existing named test passing, and prove the change is mutation-sensitive at its own decision site rather than only that the suite is green. All seat authority must continue to route through the single predicate; do not reintroduce a parallel check. Exact clean pushed head, gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check and go test ./... -count=1 green. Publish per-path artifacts at every changed stable path, resting on your promise under this request plus stable bases; do not rest them on the artifacts at d73400e8 that this head supersedes, since retiring those would stale the new artifacts on arrival. Supersede the live artifacts at the changed paths. Then request independent review naming the exact head and every artifact. Do not merge.","to":"09c97907402e7747d051c0c599d9eb463c48d19825820a3e4c433cde2e60e2a8"}}